1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:04,870
So Dalton takes his question to the
experts.

2
00:00:05,557 --> 00:00:09,047
Manchester, the last day of October, 1794.

3
00:00:10,097 --> 00:00:11,907
He moved to the town the year before.

4
00:00:12,594 --> 00:00:13,594
He's 28.

5
00:00:14,234 --> 00:00:16,824
He teaches mathematics and natural
philosophy.

6
00:00:17,511 --> 00:00:19,661
That's the old name for the study of
nature.

7
00:00:20,347 --> 00:00:22,937
And his college is called the New College.

8
00:00:24,007 --> 00:00:26,237
Tonight, he stands up in front of the
town's

9
00:00:26,287 --> 00:00:29,477
Literary and Philosophical Society and
reads out a paper.

10
00:00:30,164 --> 00:00:34,474
Its title is "Extraordinary facts relating
to the vision of colours".

11
00:00:35,161 --> 00:00:37,211
The subject is his own eyes.

12
00:00:37,898 --> 00:00:39,808
It's his first paper to the Society.

13
00:00:40,858 --> 00:00:42,608
And it's a careful paper.

14
00:00:43,295 --> 00:00:45,785
He doesn't just say that he sees colours
oddly.

15
00:00:46,472 --> 00:00:50,842
He lists them, one by one, with examples
anyone can check.

16
00:00:51,892 --> 00:00:52,902
Here's one.

17
00:00:53,748 --> 00:00:58,798
The face of a laurel leaf, he says, is a
good match to a stick of red sealing-wax.

18
00:00:59,485 --> 00:01:02,015
That's the wax people once used to seal
letters.

19
00:01:03,065 --> 00:01:04,175
Think about that.

20
00:01:04,862 --> 00:01:07,272
A green leaf and a red stick of wax.

21
00:01:07,322 --> 00:01:11,369
To you, about as different as two colours
get.

22
00:01:12,056 --> 00:01:13,586
To him, a match.

23
00:01:14,636 --> 00:01:17,446
He describes the band of colours from a
prism too.

24
00:01:18,133 --> 00:01:21,743
A prism is a triangular piece of glass
that throws colours on a wall.

25
00:01:22,430 --> 00:01:24,760
The part that others call red, he writes,

26
00:01:25,089 --> 00:01:28,919
looks to him "little more than a shade, or
defect of light".

27
00:01:29,029 --> 00:01:30,559
Almost no light at all.

28
00:01:31,246 --> 00:01:35,196
Where others see six colours, he sees two,
or at most three.

29
00:01:35,426 --> 00:01:36,336
Yellow and blue.

30
00:01:36,386 --> 00:01:39,116
Or yellow, blue and purple.

31
00:01:40,166 --> 00:01:41,856
Then he does what a scientist should.

32
00:01:41,906 --> 00:01:44,793
He offers an explanation.

33
00:01:45,843 --> 00:01:47,193
Your eye isn't hollow.

34
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:49,890
It's filled with clear fluids.

35
00:01:50,577 --> 00:01:53,567
The biggest is a clear jelly called the
vitreous humour.

36
00:01:54,254 --> 00:01:57,384
Here, humour is an old word for a fluid in
the body.

37
00:01:58,071 --> 00:02:00,801
Light passes through it on its way to the
back of the eye.

38
00:02:01,851 --> 00:02:05,901
Dalton's guess is that in his eyes, one of
these fluids isn't clear.

39
00:02:06,587 --> 00:02:07,857
It's tinted.

40
00:02:08,544 --> 00:02:11,894
In his words, "probably some modification
of blue".

41
00:02:12,581 --> 00:02:14,251
So, some kind of blue.

42
00:02:14,938 --> 00:02:17,448
And he supposes it must be the vitreous
humour.

43
00:02:17,498 --> 00:02:20,028
And honestly?

44
00:02:20,278 --> 00:02:21,348
it makes sense.

45
00:02:22,035 --> 00:02:24,485
Think of looking at the world through blue
glass.

46
00:02:25,172 --> 00:02:29,522
In Dalton's version, the tinted jelly
soaks up much of the red and the green
light.

47
00:02:29,572 --> 00:02:33,258
Before it ever reaches the back of the
eye.

48
00:02:33,945 --> 00:02:37,535
Reds and greens would both look duller and
more like each other.

49
00:02:38,222 --> 00:02:41,372
And a red stick of wax might end up
looking like a leaf.

50
00:02:42,482 --> 00:02:44,972
He has a reason for picking the jelly,
too.

51
00:02:45,659 --> 00:02:49,849
"Otherwise," he writes, "I apprehend it
might be discovered by inspection,

52
00:02:49,899 --> 00:02:51,029
which has not been done."

53
00:02:51,079 --> 00:02:56,966
In other words, a tint anywhere easier to
see would have been noticed by now.

54
00:02:58,016 --> 00:03:01,126
So the only way to check the jelly is to
open an eye.

55
00:03:01,813 --> 00:03:03,423
And Dalton only has two.

56
00:03:04,473 --> 00:03:07,363
At some point, he asks for exactly that.

57
00:03:08,050 --> 00:03:10,620
For his eyes to be examined after he dies.

58
00:03:11,306 --> 00:03:14,616
His biographer would call it his "strongly
expressed wish".

59
00:03:15,666 --> 00:03:19,756
But a blue tint only explains colours if
we know what colour is.

60
00:03:20,443 --> 00:03:22,953
And on that, Dalton leans on one man.

61
00:03:23,003 --> 00:03:26,930
A man who died long before Dalton was
born.
